from author import Bank,save_batch
banks=[]
b=Bank('topic-01','mathematics','linear-algebra','Linear Algebra · Rank, Eigenstructure & Linear Systems','002');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&2&3\\2&4&6\\1&1&1\end{pmatrix}$, find its rank.',2,'Rows (1,2,3), (2,4,6), (1,1,1)','Find rank(A).','Row reduction',r'\operatorname{rank}(A)=\text{number of pivots}',r'Row 2 equals twice row 1, so eliminate it. | Subtract row 1 from row 3 to obtain (0,−1,−2). | Rows (1,2,3) and (0,−1,−2) are independent; there are two pivots and rank is 2.','A zero determinant implies rank below 3, not necessarily rank 1.')
b.m(r'The system $x+y=2$, $2x+2y=k$ has infinitely many solutions for which value of k?','B',['0','4','2','Every real k'],'Two linear equations; parameter k','Determine the consistency condition.','Consistency and rank',r'\operatorname{rank}(A)=\operatorname{rank}([A|b])<n',r'The left side of equation 2 is twice equation 1. | Consistency therefore requires its right side to be twice 2: k = 4. | At k = 4 only one equation is independent for two unknowns, leaving infinitely many solutions.','A dependent coefficient row does not imply consistency for every right-hand side.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Let $A=\begin{pmatrix}4&1\\0&-2\end{pmatrix}$. Compute $\operatorname{tr}(A^3)$.',56,'Upper-triangular A with diagonal 4, −2','Find trace(A³).','Eigenvalue powers',r'\operatorname{tr}(A^m)=\sum_i\lambda_i^m',r'The eigenvalues of a triangular matrix are its diagonal entries, 4 and −2. | Cubing gives 64 and −8. | The trace of A³ is their sum, 64 − 8 = 56.','Do not cube trace(A); trace does not commute with nonlinear scalar operations.')
b.s(r'For $A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&2\end{pmatrix}$, which statements hold?',['A','C'],['(1,1)ᵀ is an eigenvector with eigenvalue 3','(1,−1)ᵀ has eigenvalue 3','A is positive definite','det(A) = 5'],'Real symmetric A','Select all valid eigenstructure statements.','Symmetric eigenstructure',r'Av=\lambda v,\quad A\succ0\iff\lambda_i>0',r'Multiplication gives A(1,1)ᵀ = (3,3)ᵀ and A(1,−1)ᵀ = (1,−1)ᵀ. | The two eigenvalues are 3 and 1, both positive; A is positive definite. | Their product gives det(A) = 3. Thus only A and C hold.','Do not assign the same eigenvalue to both independent eigenvectors.')
b.n(r'For $A=\begin{pmatrix}2&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}$, enter the (1,2) entry of $A^{-1}$.',-1,'A is a 2 × 2 real matrix','Find the upper-right inverse entry.','Matrix inverse',r'A^{-1}=\frac{1}{ad-bc}\begin{pmatrix}d&-b\\-c&a\end{pmatrix}',r'The determinant is 2×1 − 1×1 = 1, so the inverse exists. | The adjugate is [[1,−1],[−1,2]]. | Division by determinant 1 leaves upper-right entry −1.','The off-diagonal cofactors change sign.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'A projection matrix $P\in\mathbb R^{5\times5}$ satisfies $P^2=P$ and rank(P) = 3. Find trace(P).',3,'P² = P; rank 3; dimension 5','Find trace(P).','Idempotent matrices',r'\lambda^2=\lambda\ \Rightarrow\ \lambda\in\{0,1\}',r'Every eigenvalue obeys λ² = λ, hence it is 0 or 1. | An idempotent matrix is diagonalizable because its minimal polynomial has distinct roots. Rank 3 means three eigenvalues equal 1. | The other two equal 0, so trace(P) = 3.','Projection rank and ambient dimension are different quantities.')
b.m(r'Which matrix is orthogonal over the real numbers?','C',[r'$\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$',r'$\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&1/2\end{pmatrix}$',r'$\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$',r'$\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&0\end{pmatrix}$'],'Four candidate real 2 × 2 matrices','Choose a matrix with QᵀQ = I.','Orthogonality',r'Q^TQ=I',r'An orthogonal matrix has unit columns with zero pairwise inner product. | Candidate C has columns (0,1) and (−1,0), both unit and perpendicular. | Its QᵀQ equals I. A has nonorthogonal columns, B has nonunit columns and D is singular.','Determinant ±1 is necessary but is not sufficient by itself.')
b.n(r'In the basis $b_1=(1,1)^T$, $b_2=(1,-1)^T$, the vector $v=(6,2)^T$ has coordinates $(c_1,c_2)$. Find $c_2$.',2,'Basis vectors (1,1), (1,−1); v = (6,2)','Find the second basis coordinate.','Change of basis',r'v=c_1b_1+c_2b_2',r'Equating components gives c₁+c₂ = 6 and c₁−c₂ = 2. | Subtract the second equation from the first: 2c₂ = 4. | Therefore c₂ = 2 (and c₁ = 4), reproducing v.','Coordinates depend on the basis; do not read c₂ directly from v₂.')
b.s(r'A real 3 × 3 matrix has eigenvalues 1, 2, 4. Which values are necessarily correct?',['A','B','D'],['det(A) = 8','trace(A) = 7','rank(A) = 2','det(A⁻¹) = 1/8'],'Eigenvalues 1, 2 and 4','Select all forced matrix invariants.','Eigenvalue invariants',r'\det A=\prod_i\lambda_i,\quad\operatorname{tr} A=\sum_i\lambda_i',r'The eigenvalue product is 1×2×4 = 8 and the sum is 7. | No eigenvalue is zero, so the matrix is invertible and has rank 3. | The inverse determinant is the reciprocal, 1/8. Select A, B and D.','Rank counts all nonzero eigenvalues with algebraic multiplicity for an invertible matrix.')
b.n(r'Let $A=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}$. Find the (1,2) entry of $A^5$.',5,'A = I+N with N₁₂ = 1 and all other N entries zero','Find (A⁵)₁₂.','Nilpotent binomial expansion',r'(I+N)^m=I+mN\quad(N^2=0)',r'Compute N² = 0, so every binomial term containing N² or higher vanishes. | Thus A⁵ = I + 5N. | Its upper-right entry is 5, even though both eigenvalues of A are 1.','Repeated eigenvalues do not imply that a matrix equals the identity.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'The vectors $v_1=(1,0,1)^T$, $v_2=(0,1,1)^T$, $v_3=(1,1,k)^T$ form a basis of $\mathbb R^3$ except at one value of k. Enter that value.',2,'Three parameterized column vectors','Find the exceptional k.','Basis and invertibility',r'\{v_i\}\text{ is a basis}\iff\det[v_1\ v_2\ v_3]\ne0',r'The column matrix is [[1,0,1],[0,1,1],[1,1,k]]. | Subtract rows 1 and 2 from row 3 to produce [0,0,k−2], so the determinant is k−2. | Dependence occurs only at k = 2; there v₃ = v₁+v₂.','The question asks for the exceptional nonbasis value, not the set of valid values.',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For real square matrices A and B of the same order, which equalities always hold?',['A','D'],['trace(AB) = trace(BA)','AB = BA','det(A+B) = det(A)+det(B)','det(AB) = det(A)det(B)'],'Arbitrary equal-order square matrices','Select identities valid without extra assumptions.','Trace and determinant identities',r'\operatorname{tr}(AB)=\operatorname{tr}(BA),\quad\det(AB)=\det A\det B',r'The trace identity follows by summing aᵢⱼbⱼᵢ and interchanging indices. | Determinants are multiplicative, so D holds even when one factor is singular. | Matrix multiplication need not commute. For A = B = I₂, det(A+B) = 4 while det(A)+det(B) = 2. Select A and D.','Do not transfer scalar distributive rules to the determinant.')

b=Bank('topic-02','mathematics','calculus','Calculus · Limits, Extrema & Integration','002');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Evaluate $\lim_{x\to0}(e^{2x}-1)/x$.',2,'x tends to 0','Find the stated limit.','Taylor expansion',r'e^{u}=1+u+O(u^2)',r'Substitute u = 2x into the local exponential series. | The numerator becomes 2x + O(x²). | Division by x gives 2 + O(x), whose limit is 2.','Using e⁰−1 = 0 does not justify dividing by zero.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Find $\partial f/\partial x$ at (1,2) for $f(x,y)=x^2y+\sin y$.',4,'f(x,y) = x²y + sin(y); point (1,2)','Find the partial derivative with respect to x.','Partial differentiation',r'\frac{\partial}{\partial x}(x^2y+\sin y)=2xy',r'Hold y constant while differentiating with respect to x. | The sin(y) term has derivative zero; the other term gives 2xy. | At (1,2), the value is 2×1×2 = 4.','Do not differentiate sin(y) with respect to y in an x-partial.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Find the maximum value of $f(x)=x(4-x)$ on $0\le x\le4$.',4,'Closed interval [0,4]; f(x) = 4x − x²','Find the global maximum value.','Stationary points and endpoints',r'f\prime(x)=0,\quad f\prime\prime(x)<0',r'The derivative is 4−2x, zero at x = 2. | The second derivative is −2, so this is a local maximum; endpoint values are both 0. | f(2) = 2×2 = 4 is therefore the interval maximum.','The maximizing x and the maximum function value are different outputs.')
b.n(r'Evaluate $\int_0^1 x e^{x^2}\,dx$. Give a decimal answer.',0.8591409142295225,'Integral from 0 to 1 of x exp(x²)','Evaluate the integral.','Substitution in integration',r'u=x^2,\quad du=2x\,dx',r'The substitution u = x² changes limits 0,1 to 0,1. | The integral becomes ½∫₀¹eᵘdu = (e−1)/2. | Using e ≈ 2.718281828 gives 0.8591409142.','Remember the factor 1/2 from du = 2x dx.')
b.n(r'Evaluate $\int_0^1\int_0^x (x+y)\,dy\,dx$.',0.5,'Triangular region 0 ≤ y ≤ x ≤ 1','Evaluate the double integral.','Iterated integration',r'\int_0^x(x+y)\,dy=\frac32x^2',r'Integrate with respect to y while treating x as constant: xy+y²/2. | Evaluate y = 0 to x to obtain x²+x²/2 = 3x²/2. | The outer integral is (3/2)(1/3) = 1/2.','Do not replace the triangular region by a unit square.')
b.m(r'For $f(x)=x^2$ on [1,3], what value of c satisfies the Lagrange mean value theorem?','B',['1','2','3','4'],'Continuous and differentiable f(x) = x²; interval [1,3]','Find c in (1,3).','Mean value theorem',r'f\prime(c)=\frac{f(b)-f(a)}{b-a}',r'The secant slope is (9−1)/(3−1) = 4. | f′(c) = 2c must equal 4. | Hence c = 2, which lies inside the interval.','The theorem locates a derivative value at an interior point.')
b.s(r'Which improper integrals converge?',['A','C'],[r'$\int_1^\infty x^{-2}dx$',r'$\int_1^\infty x^{-1}dx$',r'$\int_0^1 x^{-1/2}dx$',r'$\int_0^1 x^{-2}dx$'],'Four power-law improper integrals','Select all convergent integrals.','Power-integral convergence',r'\int_1^\infty x^{-p}dx<\infty\iff p>1',r'At infinity, x⁻ᵖ is integrable exactly when p > 1, so A converges and B diverges. | Near zero, x⁻ᵖ is integrable exactly when p < 1, so C converges and D diverges. | Select A and C; the endpoint being improper changes the criterion.','Do not use the infinity criterion at the zero endpoint.')
b.n(r'Find the coefficient of $x^3$ in the Taylor series of $\ln(1+x)$ about x = 0.',0.3333333333333333,'Expansion about 0; |x| < 1','Find the cubic coefficient.','Taylor series coefficients',r'\ln(1+x)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}n x^n',r'The first terms are x − x²/2 + x³/3. | The coefficient multiplies x³; it is not the third derivative itself. | Therefore the required coefficient is 1/3 ≈ 0.3333333333.','Taylor coefficients divide the nth derivative by n!.')
b.n(r'For $f(x,y)=x^2+2y^2$ constrained by $x+y=3$, find the minimum value.',6,'Quadratic f; linear equality x+y = 3','Find the constrained minimum value.','Constrained quadratic minimization',r'f(x,3-x)=3x^2-12x+18',r'Eliminate y using y = 3−x and expand to 3x²−12x+18. | Differentiating gives 6x−12 = 0, so x = 2 and y = 1. | The second derivative is 6 > 0. The minimum value is 4+2 = 6.','The unconstrained origin is infeasible because x+y must equal 3.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'Evaluate $\int_0^{\infty}e^{-3x}\,dx$.',0.3333333333333333,'Positive decay coefficient 3','Evaluate the improper integral.','Exponential tail integration',r'\int_0^R e^{-ax}dx=\frac{1-e^{-aR}}a',r'Integrate on the finite interval [0,R] to obtain (1−e⁻³ᴿ)/3. | As R tends to infinity, the exponential term tends to zero. | The integral is 1/3.','Exponential decay, rather than growth, is needed at the infinite endpoint.',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'For $f(x,y)=x^2+y^2-2x-4y$, which statements are true?',['A','C','D'],['The only stationary point is (1,2)','The Hessian has determinant −4','The stationary point is a strict minimum','The minimum value is −5'],'Real quadratic in x,y','Classify the stationary point.','Hessian test',r'\nabla f=0,\quad H=\begin{pmatrix}2&0\\0&2\end{pmatrix}',r'The gradient equations 2x−2 = 0 and 2y−4 = 0 give (1,2). | The Hessian has positive eigenvalues 2 and 2 and determinant 4; the point is a strict minimum. | Completing squares gives f = (x−1)²+(y−2)²−5, so its minimum is −5. Select A, C and D.','A positive Hessian determinant alone does not establish a minimum without a positive leading minor.')
b.n(r'Let $F(a)=\int_0^a x^2 dx$. Find $F\prime(2)$.',4,'F is an integral with variable upper limit','Find derivative of F at 2.','Fundamental theorem of calculus',r'\frac{d}{da}\int_0^a f(x)dx=f(a)',r'The integrand x² is continuous. | By the fundamental theorem, F′(a) = a². | At a = 2, the derivative is 4.','Do not evaluate the integral at 2 when asked for its derivative.',difficulty='easy')

b=Bank('topic-03','mathematics','differential-equations','Differential Equations · Initial Values & Boundary Conditions','002');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'Solve $y\prime+2y=4$, $y(0)=0$. Enter $y(\ln2)$.',1.5,'First-order linear ODE; initial value 0','Find y at ln(2).','Integrating factor',r'y(t)=2+Ce^{-2t}',r'The constant particular solution is y = 2; the homogeneous solution is Ce⁻²ᵗ. | Apply y(0) = 0 to get C = −2, hence y = 2−2e⁻²ᵗ. | At t = ln2, e⁻²ᵗ = 1/4, so y = 2−1/2 = 1.5.','The initial condition applies to the sum of complementary and particular solutions.')
b.n(r'If $y\prime\prime+3y\prime+2y=0$, $y(0)=1$ and $y\prime(0)=0$, find the coefficient of $e^{-t}$ in y(t).',2,'Second-order homogeneous ODE; initial displacement 1 and slope 0','Find the slow-mode coefficient.','Characteristic roots and initial conditions',r'y=Ae^{-t}+Be^{-2t}',r'The characteristic polynomial factors as (r+1)(r+2). | Initial conditions give A+B = 1 and −A−2B = 0. | Subtracting yields B = −1 and A = 2, so the e⁻ᵗ coefficient is 2.','Both displacement and derivative initial conditions are required.')
b.m(r'For $y\prime\prime+4y=0$, $y(0)=0$, $y\prime(0)=2$, which solution is correct?','C',[r'$\cos(2t)$',r'$2\sin(2t)$',r'$\sin(2t)$',r'$\sin(t)$'],'Undamped ODE; y(0)=0; y′(0)=2','Choose the full initial-value solution.','Sinusoidal natural response',r'y=A\cos2t+B\sin2t',r'The characteristic roots ±2j give the stated sinusoidal form. | y(0)=0 makes A=0. Since y′(0)=2B=2, B=1. | Therefore y(t)=sin(2t); its derivative at zero is 2.','The derivative contributes the angular-frequency factor 2.')
b.n(r'For $y\prime=3t^2$ and $y(0)=2$, enter y(2).',10,'Derivative 3t²; initial value 2','Find y at t=2.','Direct integration',r'y(t)=t^3+C',r'Integrate 3t² to obtain t³ plus a constant. | The condition at zero gives C=2. | At t=2, y=8+2=10.','Do not drop the integration constant.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'On t > 0, solve $t y\prime-y=t^2$ with y(1)=0. Enter y(2).',2,'t>0; Euler-type first-order equation; y(1)=0','Find y(2).','Euler equation and substitution',r'\left(\frac yt\right)\prime=\frac{ty\prime-y}{t^2}=1',r'Divide the equation by t² to identify the derivative of y/t. | Integrate: y/t=t+C; the condition y(1)=0 gives C=−1. | Thus y=t²−t and y(2)=4−2=2.','The domain t>0 allows division by t without including a singular point.')
b.n(r'The particular solution of $y\prime\prime-y=e^t$ is sought as $y_p=Cte^t$. Find C.',0.5,'Resonant forcing eᵗ; trial Cteᵗ','Find the particular-solution coefficient.','Resonance and undetermined coefficients',r'(D^2-1)(te^t)=2e^t',r'Differentiate: yₚ′=C(1+t)eᵗ and yₚ″=C(2+t)eᵗ. | Subtract yₚ to obtain 2Ceᵗ. | Match the forcing coefficient 1: 2C=1, hence C=1/2.','A trial Ceᵗ fails because eᵗ is already a homogeneous solution.',difficulty='hard')
b.s(r'For $y\prime\prime+2y\prime+y=0$, which functions individually solve the homogeneous equation?',['A','B'],[r'$e^{-t}$',r'$te^{-t}$',r'$e^t$',r'$\sin t$'],'Repeated characteristic root −1','Select every solution function.','Repeated roots',r'(D+1)^2y=0',r'The characteristic polynomial is (r+1)². | Repeated-root theory gives independent solutions e⁻ᵗ and te⁻ᵗ; direct substitution cancels each. | eᵗ gives 4eᵗ, while sin(t) gives 2cos(t), so C and D fail. Select A and B.','Repeated roots require a t factor for the second independent solution.')
b.n(r'For $y\prime+3y=0$ and y(0)=5, find the time at which y first reaches 5/e.',0.3333333333333333,'Exponential decay; initial value 5; target 5/e','Find the first crossing time.','Decay time constant',r'y=5e^{-3t}',r'The solution is y(t)=5e⁻³ᵗ. | Set 5e⁻³ᵗ=5/e and cancel 5 to get −3t=−1. | The crossing is t=1/3. Strict monotonic decay makes it the first and only crossing.','The time constant is the reciprocal of the exponential decay rate.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Solve $y\prime\prime=6x$ with y(0)=0 and y(1)=2. Enter y(2).',10,'Boundary conditions at x=0 and x=1','Find y(2).','Boundary-value integration',r'y=x^3+C_1x+C_2',r'Two integrations give y′=3x²+C₁ and y=x³+C₁x+C₂. | y(0)=0 implies C₂=0; y(1)=2 implies C₁=1. | Therefore y(2)=8+2=10.','Conditions at distinct endpoints are boundary data, not two values at the same initial point.')
b.n(r'For the PDE $u_t=2u_x$, the initial profile is $u(x,0)=\sin x$. Enter the value of c in $u(x,t)=\sin(x+ct)$.',2,'Transport equation; sinusoidal initial profile','Find the profile translation coefficient.','Characteristic transport',r'u_t=c\cos(x+ct),\quad u_x=\cos(x+ct)',r'Substitute the proposed function into both partial derivatives. | The PDE becomes c cos(x+ct)=2 cos(x+ct), valid for all x,t only when c=2. | The initial profile is recovered at t=0, so c=2 is the required coefficient.','The profile sin(x+2t) moves left, despite its positive coefficient.')
b.m(r'For $y\prime\prime-3y\prime+2y=0$, which statement about its natural modes is correct?','D',['Both decay exponentially','There is a repeated root at −1','All modes are sinusoidal','Both characteristic roots are real and positive'],'Constant-coefficient homogeneous equation','Classify the characteristic modes.','Characteristic equation',r'r^2-3r+2=(r-1)(r-2)',r'Substitute y=eʳᵗ to get r²−3r+2=0. | Factoring gives roots 1 and 2. | Both are positive real roots; the independent natural modes grow as eᵗ and e²ᵗ. Select D.','The signs in the differential equation must be retained when forming the polynomial.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Let $y\prime=y(1-y)$ and y(0)=1/2. Find y(ln3).',0.75,'Logistic differential equation; initial value 1/2','Find the state at ln(3).','Separation of variables',r'\ln\frac{y}{1-y}=t+C',r'Separate dy/[y(1−y)] = dt and integrate to log(y/(1−y))=t+C. | Initial y=1/2 gives C=0, so y=eᵗ/(1+eᵗ). | At t=ln3 the value is 3/(1+3)=3/4.','The partial fractions integrate to log y − log(1−y), with the minus sign essential.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-04','mathematics','complex-analysis','Complex Analysis · Analyticity, Contours & Series','002');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'For $f(z)=z^2$, find the real part of $f\prime(1+j)$.',2,'Analytic polynomial z²; point 1+j','Find Re f′(1+j).','Complex differentiation',r'f\prime(z)=2z',r'The complex derivative of z² is 2z. | At z=1+j this equals 2+2j. | Its real part is 2.','The derivative is complex; select its real component only.',difficulty='easy')
b.m(r'Which function is analytic everywhere in the complex plane?','B',[r'$\overline z$',r'$z^3$',r'$|z|^2$',r'$\operatorname{Re}z$'],'Four complex-valued functions','Select the entire function.','Cauchy–Riemann conditions',r'u_x=v_y,\quad u_y=-v_x',r'A polynomial in z is complex differentiable at every finite z, so z³ is entire. | Conjugation fails the Cauchy–Riemann equations, and Re z has incompatible derivatives. | |z|² satisfies them only at an isolated point, not on an open domain. Only B is analytic everywhere.','Complex differentiability at one point does not imply analyticity on a neighborhood.')
b.n(r'For a counterclockwise circle |z|=2, let $I=\oint dz/(z-1)$. Enter the imaginary part of I.',6.283185307179586,'Simple pole at 1 inside the radius-2 circle; counterclockwise orientation','Find Im(I).','Cauchy integral formula',r'\oint_C\frac{dz}{z-a}=2\pi j',r'The point z=1 lies strictly inside the contour. | The residue is 1; positive orientation gives I=2πj. | Its imaginary part is 2π ≈ 6.283185307.','A clockwise contour reverses the sign; the prompt specifies counterclockwise.')
b.n(r'Find the residue of $1/[(z-1)(z+2)]$ at z=1.',0.3333333333333333,'Rational function; simple pole z=1','Find the residue at 1.','Simple-pole residue',r'\operatorname{Res}(f,a)=\lim_{z\to a}(z-a)f(z)',r'Cancel the pole factor z−1 after multiplying by it. | Evaluate the remaining factor 1/(z+2) at z=1. | The residue is 1/3.','Evaluate the remaining regular factor at the chosen pole, not at the other pole.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'Find the radius of convergence about z=0 of $1/(3-z)$.',3,'Function 1/(3−z); expansion center 0','Find the Taylor radius.','Nearest singularity and geometric series',r'\frac1{3-z}=\frac13\sum_{n=0}^{\infty}(z/3)^n',r'Factor the denominator to express the function as (1/3)/(1−z/3). | The geometric series converges when |z/3|<1. | The radius is therefore 3, the distance to the only pole.','The radius is not the constant term 1/3.',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'Which singularity classifications are correct?',['A','C','D'],[r'$(\sin z)/z$ has a removable singularity at 0',r'$e^{1/z}$ has a simple pole at 0',r'$1/z^3$ has a pole of order 3 at 0',r'$e^{1/z}$ has an essential singularity at 0'],'Three functions near z=0','Select valid isolated-singularity classifications.','Laurent principal part',r'f(z)=\sum_{n=-\infty}^{\infty}a_nz^n',r'sin(z)/z tends to 1 and its series contains no negative powers after cancellation, so the point is removable. | z⁻³ has a finite principal part ending at power −3, a third-order pole. | exp(1/z) has infinitely many negative-power terms, so its singularity is essential. Select A, C and D.','An exponential of a reciprocal is not a finite-order pole.')
b.n(r'For counterclockwise |z|=3, compute $J=(1/(2\pi j))\oint z^2/[(z-1)(z-2)]\,dz$.',3,'Two simple poles inside |z|=3','Find the normalized contour integral.','Residue theorem',r'\frac1{2\pi j}\oint f(z)dz=\sum\operatorname{Res}f',r'At z=1 the residue is 1²/(1−2)=−1. | At z=2 the residue is 2²/(2−1)=4. | Both poles are enclosed, so the normalized integral is −1+4=3.','Both enclosed poles contribute; enclosing a pole does not mean its residue is positive.',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'For $f(z)=e^z/z^2$, enter the residue at z=0.',1,'Second-order denominator; numerator analytic at 0','Find residue at 0.','Laurent coefficient extraction',r'e^z=1+z+z^2/2+\cdots',r'Divide the exponential series by z². | The leading terms are z⁻²+z⁻¹+1/2+⋯. | The residue is the coefficient of z⁻¹, which is 1.','A second-order pole does not make the residue equal to the z⁻² coefficient.')
b.n(r'Let $f(z)=u+jv$ be analytic with $u(x,y)=x^2-y^2$ and $v(0,0)=0$. Find v(1,2).',4,'u=x²−y²; analytic f; v(0,0)=0','Find the harmonic conjugate value.','Cauchy–Riemann reconstruction',r'v_y=u_x=2x,\quad v_x=-u_y=2y',r'Integrating vᵧ=2x in y gives v=2xy+g(x). | Enforcing vₓ=2y implies g′=0, so g is constant. | The origin condition makes it zero. Thus v(1,2)=4.','A function of x can appear during the first integration and must be checked.')
b.m(r'The principal argument of $-1+j$ in radians is:','C',[r'$\pi/4$',r'$-\pi/4$',r'$3\pi/4$',r'$-3\pi/4$'],'Complex point in quadrant II; principal argument in (−π,π]','Find the principal argument.','Complex polar angle',r'\arg z=\operatorname{atan2}(\operatorname{Im}z,\operatorname{Re}z)',r'The real part is negative and imaginary part positive, placing the point in quadrant II. | Equal magnitudes give a reference angle π/4. | The principal angle is π−π/4=3π/4, option C.','atan(y/x) alone loses the quadrant information.')
b.s(r'For counterclockwise |z|=1, which integrals are zero?',['A','D'],[r'$\oint z^2dz$',r'$\oint dz/z$',r'$\oint e^z dz/z$',r'$\oint dz/(z-2)$'],'Unit-circle contour; no pole on the contour','Select all vanishing integrals.','Cauchy theorem and residues',r'\oint_C f(z)dz=0\quad\text{if }f\text{ is analytic inside and on }C',r'z² is entire and 1/(z−2) has its only pole outside the unit circle, so A and D vanish. | 1/z has residue 1 inside, giving 2πj. | eᶻ/z also has residue e⁰=1, so C does not vanish. Select A and D.','A closed contour integral is zero only under the required analyticity conditions.')
b.n(r'For counterclockwise |z|=1, find the real value of $K=(1/(2\pi j))\oint e^{2z}/z^3\,dz$.',2,'Third-order denominator at 0; analytic numerator e²ᶻ','Find the normalized integral.','Higher-order Cauchy formula',r'\frac1{2\pi j}\oint\frac{f(z)}{z^{n+1}}dz=\frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!}',r'Here n=2 and f(z)=e²ᶻ. | The second derivative at zero is 4. | Divide by 2! to get K=4/2=2; equivalently this is the z² coefficient of e²ᶻ.','The factorial in the Cauchy derivative formula is essential.',difficulty='hard')

b=Bank('topic-05','mathematics','probability-and-statistics','Probability · Conditioning, Distributions & Moments','002');banks.append(b)
b.n(r'A fair die is rolled. Conditional on an even result, find the probability that the result exceeds 3.',0.6666666666666666,'Fair die; conditioning event {2,4,6}','Find P(result>3 | even).','Conditional probability',r'P(A|B)=P(A\cap B)/P(B)',r'Given an even result, the equally likely sample space becomes {2,4,6}. | Outcomes greater than 3 in that set are {4,6}. | The conditional probability is 2/3.','Do not retain all six outcomes after conditioning.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'For independent Bernoulli trials with success probability 0.2, find the probability of exactly two successes in three trials.',0.096,'Three independent trials; p=0.2; two successes','Find a binomial probability.','Binomial law',r'P(X=k)=\binom nk p^k(1-p)^{n-k}',r'There are C(3,2)=3 placements of the two successes. | Each arrangement has probability (0.2)²(0.8)=0.032. | Multiplying gives 3×0.032=0.096.','Include the number of possible success positions.')
b.n(r'Let X be Poisson with mean 2. Find $P(X=0)$.',0.1353352832366127,'Poisson parameter λ=2','Find the zero-count probability.','Poisson distribution',r'P(X=k)=e^{-\lambda}\lambda^k/k!',r'Use k=0 in the Poisson mass function. | λ⁰=1 and 0!=1. | The probability is e⁻² ≈ 0.1353352832.','A zero count can have positive probability even when the mean is nonzero.',difficulty='easy')
b.n(r'For an exponential lifetime with rate 0.5 s⁻¹, find its mean in seconds.',2,'Rate λ=0.5 s⁻¹','Find the mean lifetime.','Exponential mean',r'E[X]=1/\lambda',r'The exponential density is λe⁻λˣ for x≥0. | Integrating x times this density gives 1/λ. | Substitution gives 1/0.5=2 seconds.','Do not confuse rate (per second) with mean time (seconds).',difficulty='easy',unit='s')
b.n(r'If E[X]=3 and E[X²]=13, find Var(X).',4,'First moment 3; second raw moment 13','Find the variance.','Central and raw moments',r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=E[X^2]-E[X]^2',r'The squared mean is 3²=9. | Subtract it from the second raw moment. | Var(X)=13−9=4, a nonnegative value as required.','E[X²] is not the same as E[X]².',difficulty='easy')
b.s(r'Which statements about independent random variables X and Y with finite second moments hold?',['A','B','D'],['E[XY] = E[X]E[Y]','Cov(X,Y) = 0','Var(X+Y) = Var(X)Var(Y)','Var(X−Y) = Var(X)+Var(Y)'],'Independent variables; finite second moments','Select all valid moment identities.','Independence and covariance',r'\operatorname{Var}(X\pm Y)=\operatorname{Var}(X)+\operatorname{Var}(Y)\pm2\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)',r'Independence makes the mixed expectation factorize, giving zero covariance. | The covariance terms in both sum and difference variance formulas vanish. | Variances add, rather than multiply, so A, B and D hold.','Zero covariance alone does not imply independence in general.')
b.n(r'A disease has prevalence 0.01. A test is positive with probability 0.9 in diseased people and 0.1 in healthy people. Find P(disease | positive).',0.08333333333333333,'Prevalence 0.01; sensitivity 0.9; false-positive rate 0.1','Find the positive predictive probability.','Bayes theorem',r'P(D|+) = \frac{P(+|D)P(D)}{P(+|D)P(D)+P(+|D^c)P(D^c)}',r'True-positive probability is 0.9×0.01=0.009. | Total positive probability is 0.009+0.1×0.99=0.108. | The required conditional probability is 0.009/0.108=1/12≈0.0833333333.','Sensitivity is P(positive | disease), not P(disease | positive).',difficulty='hard')
b.n(r'A random variable X is uniform on [−2,4]. Find its variance.',3,'Uniform density over an interval of width 6','Find Var(X).','Uniform distribution moments',r'\operatorname{Var}(X)=(b-a)^2/12',r'The mean is (−2+4)/2=1. | The width is 4−(−2)=6. | The variance is 6²/12=3; translating the interval does not change it.','Variance depends on interval width, not on the midpoint.')
b.n(r'Let X take values 0,1,2 with probabilities 1/4,1/2,1/4. Find $E[(X-1)^2]$.',0.5,'Three values and their probabilities','Find the expected squared centered value.','Discrete expectation',r'E[g(X)]=\sum_x g(x)P(X=x)',r'The transformed values (x−1)² are 1,0,1. | Weight them by 1/4,1/2,1/4 respectively. | The result is 1/4+0+1/4=1/2.','Apply the function before averaging; g(E[X]) is generally different.')
b.m(r'For a standard normal Z, which probability equals P(Z > 1)?','D',['P(Z > −1)','P(Z < 1)','P(|Z| < 1)','P(Z < −1)'],'Standard normal density symmetric about 0','Use symmetry to identify equal tail probabilities.','Normal symmetry',r'\phi(z)=\phi(-z)',r'The density at z equals the density at −z. | Reflecting the upper tail (1,∞) through zero gives the lower tail (−∞,−1). | Their areas are equal, so D is correct. The other regions include additional central probability.','The equality relates opposite tails, not a tail and a central interval.')
b.n(r'For jointly distributed X,Y with Var(X)=4, Var(Y)=9 and Cov(X,Y)=3, find the correlation coefficient.',0.5,'Variances 4 and 9; covariance 3','Find Pearson correlation.','Normalized covariance',r'\rho_{XY}=\frac{\operatorname{Cov}(X,Y)}{\sigma_X\sigma_Y}',r'The standard deviations are √4=2 and √9=3. | Their product is 6. | Correlation is 3/6=0.5, within [−1,1].','Normalize by standard deviations, not by variances.')
b.s(r'For an exponential random variable X with rate λ > 0, which statements hold?',['A','C'],[r'$P(X>s+t|X>s)=P(X>t)$ for s,t≥0',r'$E[X]=\lambda$',r'$\operatorname{Var}(X)=1/\lambda^2$',r'$P(X<0)>0$'],'Nonnegative exponential variable; rate λ','Select all valid distribution properties.','Memorylessness and moments',r'P(X>x)=e^{-\lambda x}\quad(x\ge0)',r'Dividing the survival probabilities at s+t and s gives e⁻λᵗ, proving memorylessness. | Integrating the density gives mean 1/λ and variance 1/λ². | The distribution has support x≥0, so P(X<0)=0. Select A and C.','The parameter λ is a rate, and negative lifetimes have zero probability.',difficulty='hard')
save_batch(banks,'batch-002')
